"Mistral raises €3B to make sovereign, open-weight AI the technology frontier European open-weight AI company Mistral has raised €3 billion in a Series D funding round led by Samsung Electronics, reaching a post-money valuation of €21 billion. Mistral raised a €3 billion Series D funding round (valuing the company at approximately €21 billion) just three years after its founding, marking the largest funding round in European tech company history. Samsung Electronics led the round, with Scaleup Europe Fund, PSG Equity, and others participating as co-leads. The secured capital will be deployed toward frontier research, expanding computing infrastructure, advancing the sovereign AI full stack, and global business expansion. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla partner to bring open-source, privacy-centric, multilingual AI models to Smart Window, Firefox's AI browsing assistant. Mistral's AI model is integrated into Firefox Smart Window (Beta), Mozilla's AI browsing assistant. It is first available to users in France and North America, with support expanding to the United Kingdom and Germany later this year. It provides thorough privacy protection, including fine-tuning tailored to local languages and cultural contexts, as well as a zero data retention policy with unsaved conversations. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Cloudera and Mistral have partnered to enable enterprise customers to build and deploy specialized, customized sovereign AI while maintaining full control over their proprietary data. Mistral's AI models are integrated with Cloudera's hybrid data platform, supporting inference execution across on-premises, public/private cloud, and fully air-gapped environments. Enterprises can directly train customized models using decades of accumulated proprietary data within strictly controlled environments, maintaining full ownership of both their data and intelligence. Targeting regulated industries such as finance, manufacturing, and telecommunications, the partnership addresses the demand for 'Sovereign AI' where customers retain control over data, compute, and model operations. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. A case study of Mistral AI migrating a European energy company's complex 40,000-line legacy Fortran 77 code to modern C++ using AI agents and a numerical verification framework. Successfully converted 40,000 lines of physics simulation Fortran 77 code, which lacked test suites or documentation, into C++. Went beyond simple syntax translation by refactoring into a modular, object-oriented structure, and built a numerical equivalence verification framework to prove the accuracy of the results. Applied codebase documentation via AI agents prior to the migration, along with a structured workflow balancing agent autonomy and human review. Mistral x HUMAIN Mistral AI and HUMAIN have formed a strategic partnership to expand sovereign AI capabilities across Saudi Arabia and the broader Middle East. Cooperation on utilizing AI infrastructure, developing advanced models, building Arabic-specialized frontier models, and deploying local AI solutions Initial focus on cybersecurity and voice domains, establishing a joint go-to-market strategy targeting regulated industries Aimed at meeting demand for sovereign AI that ensures customer sovereignty across data, computing, and model operations Shared Selective Persistent Memory for Agentic LLM Systems This study explores a memory architecture that enhances efficiency and task completion rates in agentic LLM systems by selectively preserving and sharing only reusable core contexts across sessions. Proposed a selective persistent memory architecture that preserves four reusable core contexts—task specifications, data schemas, tool configurations, and output constraints—while discarding ephemeral reasoning steps. Implemented in a collaborative workspace platform by combining Git-based version control with a zero-token data refresh mechanism that updates data without reinvoking the model. Selective persistent memory achieved a 96% task completion rate, outperforming both naive full-history retention and no-memory approaches, while substantially reducing token costs and task completion time. NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 Delivers Leading Performance in MLPerf Inference v6.1 Debut NVIDIA debuted its next-generation Vera Rubin NVL72 system in MLPerf Inference v6.1, demonstrating overwhelming AI inference performance and infrastructure scaling efficiency. The Vera Rubin NVL72 system achieved up to 3.7x higher throughput compared to the existing GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark, and up to 2.5x higher on DeepSeek-R1. In an environment of 288 GPUs across four GB300 NVL72 racks, it achieved near-linear scaling efficiency of up to 99% compared to a single rack. Through comprehensive full-stack co-design across hardware and software—including enhanced Tensor Cores, Transformer Engine, NVFP4 precision, and disaggregated prefill/decode serving—it maximized throughput while reducing cost per token. Emerald AI, Google and NVIDIA Launch Alliance to Advance Flexible AI Data Centers Emerald AI, Google, and NVIDIA have launched the 'AI Energy Management Alliance (AEMA)' to build AI data centers that flexibly adjust power consumption according to power grid conditions. Emerald AI, Google, and NVIDIA have co-founded the 'AI Energy Management Alliance (AEMA)' aiming for grid-interactive flexible data centers. Promoting the securing of power flexibility to reduce grid load and cut infrastructure expansion costs by utilizing compute workload shifting, energy storage discharge, and more. Establishing standard technical requirements and operational guidelines based on measurable performance, such as response time, duration, and emergency response, agnostic to specific hardware or software. University of Manchester Uses NVIDIA Earth-2 to Forecast Air Pollution Across the UK A research team at the University of Manchester has built a model to rapidly and precisely forecast air pollution across the UK using NVIDIA's weather and climate AI framework, Earth-2. While conventional chemistry-based air pollution calculations incurred massive computing costs, the team implemented an efficient air quality forecasting workflow by adopting NVIDIA's generative climate models, Earth-2 CorrDiff and StormCast. Training was completed in just two days on the UK's national AI supercomputer Isambard-AI, and the team demonstrated that inference and workflows can also run on NVIDIA DGX Spark, a personal AI supercomputer. The technology can be applied to diverse fields, such as scenario simulations reflecting governmental environmental policy changes, preemptive health alerts for respiratory disease patients with conditions like asthma, and real-time decision support during emergencies such as wildfires. Cohere and Aleph Alpha sign agreement to become the first transatlantic sovereign AI solution Cohere and Aleph Alpha have entered into a business combination agreement to establish the first transatlantic sovereign AI solution. The combined entity will operate under the name Cohere, with dual headquarters in Toronto, Canada, and Berlin, Germany. Through this acquisition, the total workforce will expand to over 1,000 employees across both continents, and Aleph Alpha's Heidelberg office will remain an R&D center. The merger complies with regulatory and sovereignty requirements of both regions, strengthening the capability to deliver enterprise-grade sovereign AI solutions for governments and regulated industries. Cohere and OpenText partner to bring trusted agentic AI to governments and regulated industries Cohere and OpenText have formed a strategic partnership to help governments and regulated industries deploy agentic AI into practical operations. It combines OpenText's enterprise data and context with Cohere's secure agentic AI platform 'North' and enterprise AI models. It delivers intelligence capable of reasoning over corporate data and taking actions across systems while maintaining control over security, deployment, and data residency. Cohere's North platform and models will be integrated into OpenText Aviator AI agents, deployable across various environments including on-premises, private, public, and sovereign clouds. 3 days left to exhibit: Get your brand in front of VCs and high-value leads at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 This announcement encourages participation by highlighting that only three days remain until the deadline to apply for an exhibition booth at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026, aimed at attracting venture capitalists (VCs) and building business partnerships. The deadline to book an exhibition table for TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 is Friday, September 18 at 11:59 p.m. PT. The event will take place from October 13 to 15 at Moscone West in San Francisco, bringing together more than 10,000 founders, investors, and industry leaders. The exhibitor package includes a 6′ × 30″ exhibition table, 10 team passes, website and mobile app branding, access to the press list, and opportunities to participate in Deal Flow Café and investor networking. SK Hynix reportedly in talks with Intel to build memory chips in US SK Hynix is in discussions with Intel to explore cooperation options for producing its first memory semiconductors in the United States. SK Hynix stated that it is discussing options such as leasing space at Intel's Ohio plant or establishing a joint venture (JV) that includes cloud service providers, but noted that nothing has been finalized yet. SK Hynix is constructing a $3.8 billion AI semiconductor packaging and research facility in West Lafayette, Indiana, aiming for mass production in 2029. The collaboration is being pursued amid pressure from the Trump administration regarding semiconductor import tariffs and its push to expand domestic manufacturing, though reviews by the South Korean government concerning the overseas transfer of national core technologies could serve as variables. Former Infosys chief’s AI startup nabs another $53M Hang Ten Systems, an AI startup founded by former Infosys CEO Vishal Sikka, has raised an additional $53 million in funding just five weeks after securing its initial seed investment. Hang Ten Systems secured an additional $53 million following its initial $32 million, bringing its total cumulative funding to $85 million. The company supports large enterprises with AI strategy consulting and software modernization, helping them focus on requirements definition and verification rather than code writing. With the secured funds, it plans to expand its engineering, consulting, and sales teams, and pursue global hiring across regions including Europe and India. We don’t need AI regulation — leave safety to us, Nvidia’s Jensen Huang says Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang argued that AI regulation is unnecessary and that safety should be addressed through engineering and market dynamics rather than legislation. Jensen Huang stated that AI is not alien intelligence, but merely a complex computing system and software created by humans, which remains within the scope of existing laws and human control. He argued that safety is an engineering problem rather than a legal one, and that market principles will naturally prevent companies from releasing products that fail to ensure safety. He emphasized that innovation speed and safety are not mutually exclusive and that companies should advance rapidly while self-regulating, though some critics view his stance as an attempt to avoid regulation as a primary beneficiary of the AI boom. The sexy AI-powered dating app scams are here An article exposing dating app-based romance scams where criminal organizations exploit generative AI such as Claude to operate autonomous conversational personas at scale. Investigations by security researchers and journalists uncovered a fraudulent network of dating apps that pretend to be real people to induce video calls and messages. Anthropic detected over 100,000 abnormal requests per day via the Claude API and revealed at the Sleuthcon conference a scam network of approximately 28 dating apps operated by autonomous AI personas. The apps were listed on major US app stores disguised as typical matchmaking apps for real-life meetups, with no human involvement in the vast majority of conversations. A brief history of AI executives calling for regulation An analysis of the historical background and true intentions behind major corporate leaders who generate massive profits from the AI industry calling for AI regulation and pacing of development. Key AI corporate leaders, including Sam Altman of OpenAI, Dario Amodei of Anthropic, and Demis Hassabis of DeepMind, have recently publicly agreed on the need to slow down development before losing control of AI. Warnings about the dangers of machine and AI advancement and calls for regulation have a long historical context, including Samuel Butler's warning in 1863 and Alan Turing's lecture in 1951. In the modern AI field, Elon Musk started the trend of actively arguing dangers and urging regulation since 2014, calling it 'summoning the demon'. Optimal Pruning for Neural Architectures using Fisher Information Distances This paper proposes a novel geometric method for optimally pruning neural network parameters using geodesic distances via the Fisher information metric. Interpreting parameter pruning as a movement toward a hypersurface in the model space, the paper measures the actual change in the model by computing geodesic distances based on the Fisher information metric. It establishes an optimality hierarchy spanning from traditional magnitude-based pruning to advanced pruning techniques, depending on the accuracy of the geodesic distance approximation. In experiments on fully connected neural networks and Vision Transformers, the proposed approach demonstrated superior performance across accuracy and Matthews correlation coefficients compared to conventional magnitude-based pruning and local Fisher information methods. Safe Error Correction for Language Models: Frozen-Base Adjustment with Capability Preservation This study explores a methodology for adding a lightweight logit correction module on top of a frozen language model to correct output errors without degrading the performance of the base model. Proposed a technique to fix errors without sacrificing base model performance by applying a lightweight logit correction module (CRN v2) of approximately 34M parameters on top of a frozen Gemma 4 E2B model. In a 60-question CEHRI domain test, CRN v2 corrected 53.3% of the base model's errors while showing zero degradation across fundamental capability benchmarks such as MMLU and BoolQ. In contrast, the LoRA baseline model achieved a higher correction rate of 83.3% but suffered a 30-75% drop in benchmark performance, confirming the trade-off between correction and capability preservation. Position: AI Is Not Ready for Strategic Conflicts This research paper argues that language model-based wargaming simulations carry critical failure risks and should not be used for strategic decision-making without auditable safety guarantees. While language models (LMs) are useful for playing roles as agents or adjudicating scenarios in wargames, they carry the inherent risk that the model's language dictates the virtual reality itself. The paper identifies five key failure modes: decision laundering, adjudication opacity, role collapse, escalation-through-adjudication, and failure of strategic imagination. Standard benchmarks cannot guarantee safety, and the proper current use of open-ended wargames is as stress tests to identify vulnerabilities in LM agents. Calibrate, Then Route: A Measured Study of Learned Request Routing for Disaggregated LLM Serving A study on optimizing goodput and latency in disaggregated LLM serving environments through hardware calibration-based learned request routing techniques. Developed a calibrated router that estimates completion time by combining prompt length, expected output length, post-admission KV cache pressure, and SLO classes, and validated it on an environment with 8 NVIDIA A40 GPUs. The proposed calibrated router achieved a top average goodput of 0.864 under mixed and bursty traffic conditions, outperforming conventional methods such as round-robin and least-load routing. Actual hardware calibration—rather than simulator constants—is key, matching the goodput performance of round-robin requiring 7 GPUs with only 6 GPUs. Artificial intelligence and biosecurity: capabilities, threat pathways, and defense-in-depth governance This paper summarizes how artificial intelligence is transforming biological research while analyzing the biosecurity threat pathways it may create and presenting the need for defense-in-depth governance. General-purpose language models, biology foundation models, agentic systems, and automated laboratories are transforming biological research workflows, but they are also increasing biosecurity risks. Currently, AI exceeds expert-level baselines primarily in digital tasks (in-silico simulations and screening-evasion benchmarks) rather than physical tasks, while tacit knowledge and physical execution remain substantial barriers. Pointing out that general-purpose model alignment techniques do not transfer well to biological models, the paper proposes a capability threshold-based defense-in-depth governance framework to mitigate high risks while preserving beneficial uses. Managing Action Preconditions in Neuro-Symbolic RL: Three Placement Strategies for Embodied Agents This paper proposes strategies to formulate action preconditions as Bayesian networks and integrate them into three distinct placement points within the reinforcement learning loop to enhance the safety and reliability of embodied agents. Formalizes structured actions that require specific preconditions—analogous to human prior action knowledge—using Bayesian Networks (BNs) and integrates them into reinforcement learning. Compares and evaluates three strategies for integrating symbolic knowledge: a symbolic verifier applied during inference, a symbolic enforcer applied during both training and inference, and a symbolic learner integrated within the network for learning. Demonstrates solution quality and sample efficiency through experiments conducted on the ordered plan-chain benchmark (MiniGrid) and continuous manipulation benchmark (Fetch) environments. OmniHarness: Harnessing Generalizable Visual Generation via Symbolic Policy Learning This study proposes the OmniHarness framework, which abstracts verified execution traces into symbolic policies to overcome the generalization limitations of existing visual generation models. Abstracts verified execution traces into symbolic policies to learn common procedures and precondition/effect rules, which are then instantiated, adapted, and composed for new tasks. Guides mid-execution correction and error recovery through intermediate verification before task completion, continually refining policies via autonomous practice tasks and feedback while keeping model parameters frozen. Demonstrates superior performance across 6 benchmarks, 3 MLLM backbones, and 3 visual agent frameworks, offering plug-and-play reusability with existing systems. Driver Behavior Estimation at Signalized Intersections Using a Physics-Constrained Decision-Conditioned Autoregressive Transformer This study predicts drivers' stop-and-go decisions and longitudinal trajectories during signal changes at signalized intersections using a physics-constrained decision-conditioned autoregressive transformer. The analysis utilized 449 real-world driving datasets collected across various speed and distance conditions, along with centimeter-level precision GNSS, heart rate, and multi-level ride comfort evaluation data. Required deceleration was identified as a primary predictor for stop/go decisions, and a five-level ride comfort range based on human braking behavior was derived through maximum deceleration modeling. A two-stage framework was proposed that makes a stop/go decision in the first stage and generates longitudinal acceleration trajectories in the second stage using an autoregressive transformer that incorporates physical constraints such as jerk limits and target state conditions. HintMiner: Automatic Question Hints Mining From Q&A Web Posts with Language Model via Self-Supervised Learning Research on the HintMiner framework, which automatically extracts hints for user questions from online Q&A posts using language model-based self-supervised learning Proposes HintMiner, a tool that automatically generates question hints to address the issue of failing to receive timely answers on Q&A forums such as Stack Overflow Retrieves web Q&A posts and extracts hints through MiningNet, a transformer- and copy mechanism-based encoder-decoder neural network model trained via self-supervised learning using large-scale Q&A posts Conducted experiments on 60,000 Stack Overflow questions, achieving an average BLEU score of 36.17% and an average ROUGE-2 score of 36.29% POSPAN: Position-Constrained Span Masking for Language Model Pre-training This paper proposes POSPAN, a framework that integrates existing span masking methods by combining a masking position constraint distribution with a span length distribution, thereby improving language model pre-training performance. Existing span masking methods assumed a uniform distribution of span positions, which had the limitation of ignoring dependencies between spans. POSPAN combines span length distributions with position constraint distributions to provide various position-constrained span masking strategies that encompass existing methods. Across multiple NLU benchmark evaluations, POSPAN's optimal configuration consistently outperformed existing span length-based methods and vanilla MLM, with theoretical analysis validating its sound rationale. Few-Shot Degradation Is Not What It Seems: Behavioral Evidence, Representation Analysis, and a Random-Text Control Across 12 Models, 2 Tasks, and 2 Architectures This study investigates the causes of few-shot prompting degrading language model performance using a novel representation analysis metric that controls for prompt length effects. The effectiveness of few-shot prompting is highly task-dependent: across the same 12 open-weight models, it yielded a +24 pp improvement in news classification, but only a +3.4 pp gain in legal outcome prediction, with several models even showing performance degradation. The authors propose a technique that controls for representation changes caused by prompt length using random text, thereby measuring the pure shift driven by exemplar content itself (content delta). Unlike raw representation changes, the content delta strongly correlated with few-shot performance gains, demonstrating that models whose representations are restructured more by exemplar content derive greater benefits from few-shot prompting. The Functionalizer: Lossless Functional Decomposition for Subword Tokenization Research on the Functionalizer, a pre-tokenizer framework that decomposes orthographic variations and structural changes of words into a lossless operator-operand structure, reducing vocabulary size and enhancing code modeling performance. Performs fully reversible, lossless decomposition by transforming orthographic variations such as casing, diacritics, and character repetitions into Unicode PUA-based operators (opcodes) and base token (operand) streams. Reduces vocabulary slot requirements by up to 16% across six natural language and code corpora while providing complete corpus coverage, though domain-specific trade-offs exist with increased sequence lengths in natural language prose. Experiments on 25M-parameter GPT-2 scale models demonstrate significant improvements in code syntactic validity and character-level perplexity while maintaining prose consistency. Optimal Model Activation Policies for Inference Networks of Large Language Models This study proposes a graph-based inference network framework that optimizes the trade-off between cost and performance by conditionally activating multiple expert models to address the high inference cost of large language models (LLMs). Introduced a graph-based inference network framework where nodes represent diverse LLMs and links denote conditional model activations. Proved that the optimal activation policy minimizing expected inference cost subject to target performance constraints exhibits a threshold-based structure. Demonstrated significant cost reductions while preserving target performance through open-source LLM experiments, employing class-specific threshold sets for discriminative tasks and a single threshold for generative tasks. Single Document Extractive Summarization using Domination in Hypergraph A study on a novel extractive summarization technique that extracts key sentences from a single document by utilizing the dominating set concept in hypergraphs and a greedy algorithm. Constructs a single-document hypergraph by modeling sentences as nodes and keywords or named entities as hyperedges. Generates extractive summaries by applying a greedy algorithm to search for dominating sets within the hypergraph. Aims to validate performance by comparing the proposed approach with existing state-of-the-art graph-based text summarization methods. Latent Undertow: How Ordinary Typos Break Probes This study explores how everyday typos significantly degrade the performance of malicious prompt detection probes that inspect LLM hidden states, and proposes methods to address this issue. While LLMs themselves handle typos flexibly, probes that analyze hidden states to detect malicious prompts suffer a sharp drop in detection rates due to vector rotation caused by minor typos. When roughly three common typos occur per message, a single-position probe's performance drops by 12.0 percentage points at TPR@FPR=1%, and even multi-position aggregation cannot completely prevent this performance degradation. By introducing a 'KV-cache fork' technique that appends a short fixed suffix after user messages to leverage the rapid spatial decay characteristics of typo perturbations, the researchers recovered 95% of the performance gap. Mistral x Mozilla: Privacy-Preserving Multilingual AI Browsing Mistral and Mozilla have partnered to integrate Mistral models into the beta of Smart Window, Firefox's AI browsing assistant, reflecting privacy protection as well as regional linguistic and cultural contexts. Mistral models have been integrated into the beta of Smart Window, Firefox's AI browsing assistant, enabling understanding of complex queries, recall of past browsing history, and tab-based information retrieval. Conversations are not stored on Mozilla servers by default, and partners including Mistral have agreed to zero data retention, ensuring user privacy and control. Through fine-tuning that reflects local languages, dialects, and cultural contexts, localized tailored AI experiences and open-source-based sovereign AI are expanded to general consumers. Show GN: ABTO: A/B Testing LLM Models by Comparing Real User Conversion Rates and Costs Introducing ABTO, an A/B testing tool that routes production service traffic across multiple LLM models to combine, compare, and analyze call costs alongside user conversion and behavioral metrics Goes beyond simple benchmarks or response quality evaluations by directly linking and measuring call costs per model against actual business conversion metrics such as saves, shares, and purchases by users Ensures service reliability through a Go-based Gateway with multi-level fallback/failover architectures, and enables seamless integration simply by configuring the OpenAI SDK's base_url and headers Allows teams to freely set traffic split ratios and compare feature-by-feature performance, empowering quantitative, data-driven decisions for selecting the most cost-effective LLM Show GN: Odeduk - An AI Tool That Delves into Public Data, Even Applying for APIs on Your Behalf An open-source tool called 'Odeduk' has been released, allowing an AI agent to automatically handle everything from exploring public data and applying for API access to obtaining authentication keys and querying data. The AI agent automates the cumbersome process of searching for data, reviewing specifications, applying for API access, confirming approval, and making actual API calls. Provided as a Go-based CLI and MCP (Model Context Protocol), it can interface with various models such as Codex and Claude, while keeping authentication keys private from the model for security. It autonomously connects and explores public data across different organizations to derive interesting insights, making it useful for planners and developers conducting PoC validations. Why LLM Outlook Remains Pessimistic Even After Navier-Stokes An analysis suggesting that even if LLMs demonstrate remarkable performance, full automated replacement of knowledge work remains difficult due to the challenges of rigorous specification and verification, as well as the bottleneck of human review. Despite outstanding benchmark achievements such as solving Navier-Stokes, current LLMs remain vulnerable to slight variations outside their training distribution and pose significant risks of reward hacking. Writing rigorous formal specifications and performing verification to prevent reward hacking is far more costly than implementation itself, making it difficult to apply across most knowledge work due to a shortage of domain experts. Review by human experts also cannot keep pace with the massive volume of LLM outputs, ultimately remaining the core productivity bottleneck and making fully autonomous replacement unrealistic. Building a Linux GPU Driver for M4 Mac Mini in One Month Covers the process and achievements of developers Cody Ho and Niklas, who developed an OpenGL ES 3.0-compliant Linux GPU driver for the M4 Mac Mini in about a month using AI tools and clean-room reverse engineering. Reverse-engineered the AGX firmware ABI via a clean-room approach based on hypervisor tracing and replay without analyzing Apple binaries. Actively leveraged AI tools such as Codex and Claude to implement user-space drivers and Linux kernel drivers in a short period. Achieved WebGL execution in Chrome/Firefox and reached 200 fps in Minecraft on the M4 Mac Mini, though further verification is required for official distribution. LARA: small, composable behaviours for frozen LLMs [P] An introduction to LARA, a framework that enables composing and routing various behaviors at the token level by applying modular low-rank residual adapters to frozen large language models (LLMs). Individual behavior modules can be kept compact and independent by training low-rank residual adapters on specific layers without directly modifying the base model weights. Through a Mixture of Behaviors (MoB) approach utilizing soft routers, multiple behaviors can be selected or blended at the token level, allowing them to operate within a single model. A PyTorch library is currently open-sourced, complete with training code, examples, comparisons with LoRA, and author-specific style modules. PhD in ML in the AI era, autoresearch [D] A post by a student contemplating the viability of pursuing an academic PhD in machine learning amid the rapid pace of AI advancement, infrastructure disparities, and the emergence of automated research technologies. Concerns are raised that academia is falling behind the industry due to massive infrastructure demands and the rapid pace of progress, unless one belongs to a well-funded, large-scale laboratory. It highlights that while research standards continue to rise alongside an explosion in conference paper submissions, the compensation and treatment of academic PhD students remain low relative to their capabilities. With the emergence of automated research (autoresearch) technology creating uncertainty around the future roles and opportunities for researchers, the author seeks advice on whether entering industry early to generate income would be a better path. Why I'm Still Bearish on LLMs After Navier-Stokes A critical analysis arguing that despite the remarkable achievements of state-of-the-art LLMs, fully replacing knowledge workers remains unlikely due to limitations in generalization capabilities and the prohibitive costs of rigorous specification and verification. While the soaring valuations of cutting-edge AI labs rely on the narrative of full automation, real-world models still fail or resort to reward hacking even with minor shifts outside their training distribution, requiring continuous supervision. Writing rigorous specifications to prevent reward hacking requires scarce expertise and entails immense costs, potentially causing the labor cost of specification and verification to exceed that of direct implementation. Solving pure mathematical challenges such as the Navier-Stokes equations represents a best-case scenario with decades of validated specifications and audited provers like Lean, making it difficult to generalize to everyday work environments. How chimps teach their kids tool tricks A study finding that adult chimpanzees teach tool skills to young individuals through social learning by demonstrating tool use and handing over the tools. Adult chimpanzees intentionally demonstrate tool-use behaviors and then pass the tools to their young to facilitate observational learning. Mother chimpanzees serve as the primary educators, with observation, supervised practice, and tool transfer functioning as a form of education. It has been reaffirmed that tool use, once considered uniquely human, has been passed down across generations and evolved into diverse forms within chimpanzee society as well. I’ve used both iPhone 18 Pro models – here’s how my buying advice is changing in 2026 A buying guide analysis explaining why the smaller Pro model is now recommended after hands-on experience with the iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max, as functional differences between them have virtually disappeared. Aside from screen and battery size, major features—including the triple-camera system with dual/variable aperture, durability, and iOS 27 software—are nearly identical between the iPhone 18 Pro and Pro Max. For the first time this year, a 2TB storage option is available on the smaller Pro model, eliminating the need to choose the Pro Max solely for capacity. Due to the increased number of physical buttons (Camera Control, Action button, etc.), the 6.3-inch Pro model offers superior one-handed usability and ergonomics. Windows 11 out-of-band update fixes audio glitch and other bugs – grab it now Microsoft has rolled out an emergency out-of-band update for Windows 11 to resolve audio outages, major security vulnerabilities, and other issues that emerged following the September regular update. Following the September 8 regular update, errors occurred where USB Audio Class 1.0 devices based on legacy drivers produced no sound or were unable to adjust volume. This emergency patch addresses not only the audio glitch but also security vulnerabilities involving system privilege hijacking (CVE-2026-62721) and local privilege escalation (CVE-2026-85921). Remote Desktop Services (RDS) login failures and backend freeze issues, as well as Hyper-V virtual machine folder sharing problems, have also been resolved through this update. Dropbox Evolves Riviera Content Processing Platform to Support AI Workloads Dropbox has evolved Riviera from a file preview service into a general-purpose content processing and AI workload platform that supports over 300 formats and more than 100 transformation capabilities. Riviera has expanded from a simple preview tool into a general-purpose processing platform with decoupled orchestration and execution, alongside modular transformation pipelines. It handles hundreds of thousands of transformations per second, supporting services such as Search, Replay, Sign, and Dash, as well as text and embedding transformations and RAG workflows for large language models (LLMs). Dropbox has begun offering asynchronous Markdown conversion, audio/video transcription, and metadata extraction capabilities to external developers via public APIs and open-source integration tools. Article: Your Next DSL Author Is a Language Model To resolve the issue of LLMs hallucinating DSL syntax, this article proposes 'Typed Domain Grounding,' an approach that embeds domains as typed internal DSLs within host languages that have abundant training data. The fundamental cause of LLMs hallucinating syntax in specific DSLs is not a lack of knowledge, but rather low occurrence frequency in the training data. Because RAG only supplements factual relationships and cannot complement the expression syntax itself, 'Typed Domain Grounding (TDG)'—in the form of an internal DSL leveraging the type systems of data-rich host languages like Python or TypeScript—serves as an effective solution. Benchmark results with Claude Sonnet 5 and GPT-4o demonstrated that using a compiler as a strict error-verification oracle achieved higher structural accuracy and lower hallucination rates compared to external DSLs. Presentation: Teaching Engineers, Trusting AI: How Education Enabled Autonomous Code Review Covers how Duolingo combined developer education with AI safety guardrails to automate the code review process without increasing defect rates. Going beyond mere tool adoption, Duolingo's DevEx AI team supports engineers in effectively utilizing AI through internal AI literacy workshops and observability dashboards. By introducing an automated PR risk assessment bot to redesign the code review process, they accelerated software deployment speeds without increasing defect rates. For successful AI adoption within an organization, providing tailored education and safety mechanisms to overcome engineers' skepticism is essential. Dropbox Outlines How Focusing on Existing Infrastructure Efficiency Can Create Headroom for AI Dropbox shared how it accommodates growing AI demand by maximizing the efficiency of its existing infrastructure rather than relying solely on expanding new data centers. It is resolving capacity constraints by directly managing all layers—from software to racks, power, and cooling—through its Magic Pocket storage system and colocation data centers. It reduces idle power consumption and prevents hotspots through its Deep Sleep system, which places idle servers and disks into standby mode, alongside workload redistribution. It has optimized infrastructure by introducing Shingled Magnetic Recording (SMR) technology to increase storage density and extending hardware lifespans by analyzing actual failure rates instead of relying on fixed replacement cycles. Attackers Exploit WooCommerce Wholesale Lead Capture Flaw to Plant PHP Web Shells Attackers are actively exploiting critical vulnerabilities in the WooCommerce Wholesale Lead Capture and The Events Calendar plugins to plant PHP web shells and achieve remote code execution. A missing file validation vulnerability in the WooCommerce Wholesale Lead Capture plugin (CVE-2026-27540, CVSS 9.8) allows unauthenticated attackers to upload malicious PHP web shells and execute arbitrary code remotely. Wordfence has blocked over 100,000 exploit attempts targeting the vulnerability since June 2026, with 99 attempts detected within the past 24 hours alone. Two critical vulnerabilities (CVE-2026-78159, CVE-2026-78006) allowing remote code execution and complete site takeover without administrator approval were also discovered in The Events Calendar plugin, installed on over 600,000 sites, and patches have been released. \"Account Suspended After Connecting Third-Party Model to Claude Code\"... Anthropic Clarifies \"No Bans\" Covers the controversy surrounding a developer whose account was suspended after connecting a third-party model to Anthropic's coding agent 'Claude Code', alongside Anthropic's official clarification. Controversy arose after a developer reported their account was suspended following the use of a proxy to connect an OpenAI model to Claude Code. Anthropic clarified that using development tools like Claude Code in conjunction with third-party models is not prohibited. Developers continue attempting to pair Claude Code's tool environment with various cost-effective models to reduce token costs and optimize tasks. Google Adds Personalized Learning Features to 'Gemini Notebook'...'Study by Listening and Speaking' Google has significantly expanded personalized learning features in its AI learning tool 'Gemini Notebook'—including real-time voice conversations, customized quizzes, and short-form videos—while broadening free benefits for college students. Supports real-time voice conversations based on user study materials and lecture audio recording across approximately 100 languages in the mobile app Introduces interactive study guides combining summaries, infographics, quizzes, and flashcards, along with 60-second short-form video overviews supporting over 80 languages Provides college students in the U.S. and more than 140 global markets with a free one-year paid AI plan and increased usage limits \"Contractors Reviewing ChatGPT Conversations\"... OpenAI Faces Privacy Controversy Over 'Project Lily' Controversy over personal information exposure and privacy infringement has erupted as it was revealed that OpenAI operated an undisclosed initiative, 'Project Lily', deploying outsourced contract workers to review and evaluate actual ChatGPT conversations. OpenAI conducted 'Project Lily', a confidential project deploying hundreds of outsourced contractors as 'prompt reviewers' to evaluate the quality, tone, and other aspects of ChatGPT responses. Although conversations are anonymized, concerns were raised regarding the risk of sensitive personal details such as names and locations being exposed to reviewers without proper filtering during short conversations or user memory summarization processes. Beyond OpenAI, major AI companies including Google and Anthropic also disclose the possibility of human review for model performance improvement (RLHF), raising debate over whether users are clearly aware of this practice. Agility Unveils Next-Gen Humanoid 'Digit 5' That Stops When Humans Approach Agility Robotics has unveiled 'Digit 5', a next-generation humanoid robot featuring significantly enhanced performance and safety features designed for safe collaboration in human-shared workspaces. By introducing a real-time collaborative safety system that decelerates, stops, or crouches down when a person approaches to prevent collisions, on-site deployment is now possible without separate safety fencing. A human-like leg architecture increases payload capacity to 50 lbs (approx. 23 kg), while 9-minute rapid charging and swappable hands enable continuous operation of over 20 hours per day. Having accumulated 65,000 operational hours and secured $300 million in orders, the company is pursuing a public listing via a SPAC merger, with Digit 5 scheduled for rollout in North America starting the first half of 2027. Salesforce and NVIDIA Unveil 'Koa', a Reasoning Model Dedicated to CRM... \"Reducing Reliance on Big Tech\" Salesforce collaborated with NVIDIA to unveil 'Koa', its first proprietary reasoning model specialized for CRM tasks, reducing reliance on external frontier models. Salesforce launched 'Koa', its proprietary enterprise reasoning model combining 27 years of CRM expertise based on NVIDIA's open-weights model 'Nemotron'. To prevent leaks of real customer data, it was trained on synthetic data simulating business scenarios across more than 14 industries, enhancing security and cost efficiency. Deployed on its proprietary Agentforce platform, it reduces reliance on external models while maintaining partnerships with OpenAI and Anthropic. RedMagic Astra 2 Officially Launches in South Korea Featuring Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 and as the First Liquid-Cooled Tablet RedMagic has announced the official South Korea launch of the 'RedMagic Astra 2', a gaming tablet equipped with the Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 and the first liquid cooling system. Equipped with the Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 and a dedicated RedCore R4 gaming chip, delivering outstanding processing performance and intelligent power management. Features a 9.06-inch 2.4K OLED display supporting a tablet-first 185Hz high refresh rate alongside a 300Hz touch sampling rate. Applies the AquaCore Cooling System 2.0 liquid cooling technology combining Liquid Metal 3.0 and a VC vapor chamber, along with an 8300mAh battery. Trump Calls AI Extinction Warnings a 'Hoax' as US Congressional Regulatory Legislation Stalls US congressional legislation on AI safety regulations has reached a deadlock after US President Donald Trump dismissed warnings of AI causing human extinction as a hoax and voiced opposition to regulations. President Donald Trump strongly opposed AI regulatory bills, labeling claims of AI destroying humanity as a 'HOAX.' The passage of AI safety-related bills in Congress has effectively become uncertain due to opposition from the majority Republican Party and the recess schedule ahead of the November midterm elections. While the White House and Republicans emphasize corporate self-regulation and safety responsibility, Democrats and civil society experts are pushing back, expressing concern over the absence of statutory regulations at the congressional level. Testmu AI Launches Assurance Lifecycle in Kane CLI, Turning Requirement Documents into Verifiable Test Coverage Testmu AI has released the 'Assurance Lifecycle' for Kane CLI, which designs tests and tracks and maintains verification coverage based on requirement documents such as PRDs and specifications. It snapshots product specifications and requirement documents to extract use cases, automatically designing executable tests based on them. Each test is permanently linked to requirements to measure coverage based on verified requirements rather than simple test pass counts, while logging auditable evidence. It automatically adjusts test suites when requirements change, and all data is stored append-only on local disk rather than servers, integrating with CI and AI agent workflows."